// CSD-Bridge vehicle turning path conventions, used by AITURN (the Turn path ribbon button)
// and Claude's draw_turn_path tool.
//
// Vehicles are AS/NZS 2890.1:2004 Appendix B base dimensions (Figures B1 and B2), in metres:
//   length / width            overall body
//   wheelbase, front_overhang, rear_overhang
//   effective_*               the rounded corners that actually bound the swept path
//   track                     wheel centre to wheel centre (scaled from the figures; the
//                             standard doesn't dimension it). It only moves the wheel paths and
//                             sets how the nominated radius (outer front wheel) relates to the body.
//   min_turn_radius           smallest template radius, at the outer front wheel (B3.1)
//   clearance_inside/outside  B3.2: 300 mm manoeuvring both sides; B99 adds 300 mm circulation
//                             clearance on the outside
//   ground_clearance          Appendix C / B5: 120 mm under the body between the axles
//   approach/departure_angle  B5: the underside rises at 10 degrees over the overhangs
// Add your own vehicles (a service vehicle, say) as more entries with the same fields.
//
// Trucks (Austroads AP-G34-23, Design Vehicles and Turning Path Templates Guide, Edition 4.0,
// Figures 2.3, 2.4, 2.7, 2.9 and 2.10): the body is square, so effective_* is left out. With a
// group of rear axles, wheelbase and overhangs are measured from the centre of the group.
//   min_turn_radius           the smallest radius the guide publishes a template for, at the outer
//                             front wheel: 9 m for the service vehicle, 12.5 m for the rest
//   clearance_inside/outside  0.5 m from the swept path to kerbs, edge lines and objects (3.4.1)
//   max_articulation_deg      70 degrees between coupled units (3.5)
//   rear_axles, axle_spacing  the rear axle group, as drawn
// Not dimensioned in the guide's figures, so scaled from them (they don't move the swept path):
//   track 2.25 (single tyres 250 mm wide, flush with the body), rear_track / trailer track 1.98
//   (dual tyres), a prime mover's rear_overhang 1.2 (the back of its drive wheels, under the
//   trailer) and an A-trailer's rear_overhang 1.85 (the back of its wheels, under the B-trailer).
//
// Trailers: a vehicle that tows gets "hitch" (its fifth wheel, metres ahead of the centre of its
// rear axle group; negative = behind it) and "trailers", listed front to back. Each trailer has:
//   width
//   front_overhang     body ahead of the coupling: the kingpin's setback from the trailer's front
//   wheelbase          from its coupling (kingpin) back to the centre of its axle group
//   rear_overhang      from the centre of its axle group to its rear
//   hitch              where the next trailer couples on, ahead of this one's axle group (B-double: 0)
//   axles, axle_spacing, track   its axle group, as drawn
// A vehicle with trailers is driven forwards only.
//
// Layers: {vehicle} in a layer name is replaced by the vehicle's name, e.g.
// "AE CIV PLN - {vehicle} SWEPT PATH". layer_styles apply only when a layer doesn't exist yet;
// existing layers are never changed. The AITURN dialog can edit all of this per run and save it.
//
// To use your own copy, set "turn_paths_file" in %APPDATA%\CsdBridge\settings.json. A copy saved
// from the dialog lives at %APPDATA%\CsdBridge\turn-paths.json and takes precedence over this file.
